  "text": "You just bet $10 on red and lost it. Fortunately, some guru online told you about a guaranteed win strategy where each time you lose, you just double the bet and place it on the same color. And sooner or later, that color will hit and you will make all your money back plus profit. So to get back those $10, you bet $20 and still lose it. Now to regain that, you bet $40. Ah, tough luck. $80. Ah, so close. $160. This is the one. No, it's not. $320. Ah, it's just right. $320. $320. You've never seen a black streak so long. Surely it can't go for any longer. Your previous bet was $1200. So to recoup that loss you will have to dig into your savings and bet your final $2500. You're in too deep to stop now. The bets are locked, dealer spins the wheel and... This is exactly what can go wrong if you follow the martingale betting strategy. The strategy assumes that eventually you will hit red, and it's not wrong. In fact, you are pretty much guaranteed to hit red over an infinite number of spins. Problem is that we don't have infinite bankroll to supply these streaks. Even if we did, casinos have a bet limit. Okay, but real life isn't infinite. These unlucky streaks end sooner or later. It's a matter of time until I recoup my money. Yeah, but most people don't realize how even short unlucky streaks can bankrupt them, since your bet amount increases exponentially. So unless you've got literal infinite money, maybe Martingale is not for you. And if you do have infinite money, I don't know why would you be gambling?"
